    "line": 78,
    "summary": "trailReportFor can return post=true with no comments and empty prose, so postReviewToTrail fails with \"no review output to post\" instead of saying nothing was posted.",
    "failure_scenario": "A single reviewer (or every reviewer when no judge runs) outputs only `{\"comments\":null}` or `{\"summary\":\"\",\"comments\":\"none\"}`. splitTrailJSON returns prose=\"\" and ok=false, so the run goes into `unstructured`. combinedReviewNarratives then calls splitTrailJSON on the same JSON-only text, gets \"\" and skips the run, leaving Prose empty. post is still true (len(unstructured)>0), and the user sees \"Could not post the review to the trail: no review output to post\". The judge path has the same gap at line 61 when the fallback narratives are also JSON-only."
  },
  {
    "file": "cmd/entire/cli/review/trail_report.go",
    "line": 65,
    "summary": "When a reviewer's JSON line has comments, only those comments are posted and the prose bullets are dropped, even when the prose lists more findings. This breaks the code's own rule that a misbehaving model should cost an extra finding, not a lost one.",
    "failure_scenario": "The judge (line 65) or a reviewer (line 75) writes three `- [high] ...` bullets but puts only one comment in the JSON line. ok is true and len(comments)>0, so TrailReport{Comments: comments} is posted with no Prose. Two real high-severity findings never reach the trail. The contradiction check only runs when the comments array is empty."
